{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"set -euo pipefail\nspace='/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics'\nprintf '%s\\\\n' '--- records ---'\nfind \\\"\"'$space/sources/records\" -maxdepth 1 -type f -print -exec sh -c '\"'echo \\\"--- \"'$1 ---\"; sed -n \"1,220p\" \"$1\"'\"' sh {} \\\\;\nprintf '%s\\\\n' '--- claims files ---'\nfind \\\"\"'$space/claims\" -maxdepth 1 -type f -print -exec sh -c '\"'echo \\\"--- \"'$1 ---\"; sed -n \"1,220p\" \"$1\"'\"' sh {} \\\\;\nprintf '%s\\\\n' '--- relations files ---'\nfind \\\"\"'$space/relations\" -maxdepth 1 -type f -print -exec sh -c '\"'echo \\\"--- \"'$1 ---\"; sed -n \"1,220p\" \"$1\"'\"' sh {} \\\\;\nprintf '%s\\\\n' '--- source headings ---'\nrg -n '\"'^[[:space:]]{0,80}([A-Z][A-Z /-]{4,}|BOX [0-9])'\"' \\\"\"'$space/sources/artifacts/source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2/source.md\" | head -80'","aggregated_output":"/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/claims/claim-the-largest-random-circuits-combined-53-qubits-1-113-single-qubit-gates-430-two--c8ece98aae87.json\n--- /Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/claims/claim-the-largest-random-circuits-combined-53-qubits-1-113-single-qubit-gates-430-two--c8ece98aae87.json ---\n{\n  \"added_at\": \"2026-04-26T15:59:28Z\",\n  \"claim_id\": \"claim-the-largest-random-circuits-combined-53-qubits-1-113-single-qubit-gates-430-two--c8ece98aae87\",\n  \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n    \"Our largest random quantum circuits have 53 qubits, 1,113 single-qubit gates, 430 two-qubit gates, and a measurement on each qubit, for which we predict a total fidelity of 0.2%.\"\n  ],\n  \"schema_version\": \"claim_record_v1\",\n  \"short_title\": \"Large circuits remain measurable\",\n  \"source_id\": \"source-02-quantum-supremacy-using-a-programmable-superconducting-processor--f98990eb22d8\",\n  \"text\": \"The largest random circuits combined 53 qubits, 1,113 single-qubit gates, 430 two-qubit gates, and one measurement per qubit, for which the authors predicted a total fidelity of 0.2%.\"\n}\n/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/claims/claim-nisq-devices-are-best-understood-as-experimental-tools-for-exploring-many-body-q--fde7f9646dbb.json\n--- /Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/claims/claim-nisq-devices-are-best-understood-as-experimental-tools-for-exploring-many-body-q--fde7f9646dbb.json ---\n{\n  \"added_at\": \"2026-04-26T15:52:46Z\",\n  \"claim_id\": \"claim-nisq-devices-are-best-understood-as-experimental-tools-for-exploring-many-body-q--fde7f9646dbb\",\n  \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n    \"Physicists are excited about this NISQ technology, which gives us new tools for exploring the physics of many entangled particles.\",\n    \"NISQ is not likely to change the world all by itself. Instead, the primary goal for near-term quantum platforms should be to pave the way for bigger payoffs.\"\n  ],\n  \"schema_version\": \"claim_record_v1\",\n  \"short_title\": \"NISQ enables quantum exploration\",\n  \"source_id\": \"source-01-quantum-computing-in-the-nisq-era-and-beyond--2ce59b8ba8bb\",\n  \"text\": \"NISQ devices are best understood as experimental tools for exploring many-body quantum physics and as stepping stones toward more powerful quantum technologies, not as near-term world-changing machines.\"\n}\n/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/claims/claim-agreement-among-full-patch-elided-and-predicted-fidelities-on-classically-verifi--c34d4d1d9279.json\n--- /Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/claims/claim-agreement-among-full-patch-elided-and-predicted-fidelities-on-classically-verifi--c34d4d1d9279.json ---\n{\n  \"added_at\": \"2026-04-26T15:59:28Z\",\n  \"claim_id\": \"claim-agreement-among-full-patch-elided-and-predicted-fidelities-on-classically-verifi--c34d4d1d9279\",\n  \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n    \"The predicted, patch and elided fidelities all show good agreement with the fidelities of the corresponding full circuits, despite the vast differences in computational complexity and entanglement.\"\n  ],\n  \"schema_version\": \"claim_record_v1\",\n  \"short_title\": \"Verification supports extrapolation\",\n  \"source_id\": \"source-02-quantum-supremacy-using-a-programmable-superconducting-processor--f98990eb22d8\",\n  \"text\": \"Agreement among full, patch, elided, and predicted fidelities on classically verifiable circuits supports using elided circuits to estimate fidelity in the supremacy regime.\"\n}\n/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/claims/claim-current-two-qubit-gate-and-measurement-error-rates-imply-that-uncorrected-nisq-c--68f47af68b82.json\n--- /Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/claims/claim-current-two-qubit-gate-and-measurement-error-rates-imply-that-uncorrected-nisq-c--68f47af68b82.json ---\n{\n  \"added_at\": \"2026-04-26T15:52:46Z\",\n  \"claim_id\": \"claim-current-two-qubit-gate-and-measurement-error-rates-imply-that-uncorrected-nisq-c--68f47af68b82\",\n  \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n    \"With the best hardware we have now for controlling trapped ions or superconducting circuits, the error rate per gate for two-qubit gates is above the .1% level (and often much worse).\",\n    \"With these noisy devices we don\\u2019t expect to be able to execute a circuit that contains many more than about 1000 gates.\"\n  ],\n  \"schema_version\": \"claim_record_v1\",\n  \"short_title\": \"Gate errors cap circuits\",\n  \"source_id\": \"source-01-quantum-computing-in-the-nisq-era-and-beyond--2ce59b8ba8bb\",\n  \"text\": \"Current two-qubit gate and measurement error rates imply that uncorrected NISQ circuits cannot grow much beyond about one thousand fundamental two-qubit operations before noise overwhelms the signal.\"\n}\n/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/claims/claim-nisq-computers-with-roughly-50-to-a-few-hundred-qubits-mark-a-classically-hard-s--2d5ec194bedf.json\n--- /Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/claims/claim-nisq-computers-with-roughly-50-to-a-few-hundred-qubits-mark-a-classically-hard-s--2d5ec194bedf.json ---\n{\n  \"added_at\": \"2026-04-26T15:52:46Z\",\n  \"claim_id\": \"claim-nisq-computers-with-roughly-50-to-a-few-hundred-qubits-mark-a-classically-hard-s--2d5ec194bedf\",\n  \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n    \"50 qubits is a significant milestone, because that\\u2019s beyond what can be simulated by brute force using the most powerful existing digital supercomputers.\",\n    \"Noisy emphasizes that we\\u2019ll have imperfect control over those qubits; the noise will place serious limitations on what quantum devices can achieve in the near term.\"\n  ],\n  \"schema_version\": \"claim_record_v1\",\n  \"short_title\": \"Noise limits NISQ depth\",\n  \"source_id\": \"source-01-quantum-computing-in-the-nisq-era-and-beyond--2ce59b8ba8bb\",\n  \"text\": \"NISQ computers with roughly 50 to a few hundred qubits mark a classically hard simulation regime, but noisy gates place serious near-term limits on what they can compute.\"\n}\n/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/claims/claim-linear-cross-entropy-benchmarking-provides-a-fidelity-measure-by-comparing-exper--4abac38b173d.json\n--- /Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/claims/claim-linear-cross-entropy-benchmarking-provides-a-fidelity-measure-by-comparing-exper--4abac38b173d.json ---\n{\n  \"added_at\": \"2026-04-26T15:59:28Z\",\n  \"claim_id\": \"claim-linear-cross-entropy-benchmarking-provides-a-fidelity-measure-by-comparing-exper--4abac38b173d\",\n  \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n    \"F_XEB = 2^n <P(x_i)>_i - 1, where n is the number of qubits, P(x_i) is the probability of bitstring x_i computed for the ideal quantum circuit, and the average is over the observed bitstrings.\"\n  ],\n  \"schema_version\": \"claim_record_v1\",\n  \"short_title\": \"XEB measures circuit fidelity\",\n  \"source_id\": \"source-02-quantum-supremacy-using-a-programmable-superconducting-processor--f98990eb22d8\",\n  \"text\": \"Linear cross-entropy benchmarking provides a fidelity measure by comparing experimentally observed bitstrings with ideal probabilities computed by classical simulation.\"\n}\n/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/claims/claim-hybrid-qaoa-and-vqe-approaches-are-plausible-nisq-era-testbeds-but-their-ability--31ec67369767.json\n--- /Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/claims/claim-hybrid-qaoa-and-vqe-approaches-are-plausible-nisq-era-testbeds-but-their-ability--31ec67369767.json ---\n{\n  \"added_at\": \"2026-04-26T15:52:46Z\",\n  \"claim_id\": \"claim-hybrid-qaoa-and-vqe-approaches-are-plausible-nisq-era-testbeds-but-their-ability--31ec67369767\",\n  \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n    \"Will NISQ technology running QAOA or VQE be able to outperform classical algorithms that find approximate solutions to the same problems? Nobody knows.\",\n    \"The classical methods we use to solve these problems are well honed after decades of development.\"\n  ],\n  \"schema_version\": \"claim_record_v1\",\n  \"short_title\": \"NISQ speedups remain uncertain\",\n  \"source_id\": \"source-01-quantum-computing-in-the-nisq-era-and-beyond--2ce59b8ba8bb\",\n  \"text\": \"Hybrid QAOA and VQE approaches are plausible NISQ-era testbeds, but their ability to outperform mature classical approximation methods remains unresolved.\"\n}\n/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/claims/claim-highly-entangled-quantum-dynamics-is-the-most-natural-arena-for-meaningful-quant--3b6f2f5ba268.json\n--- /Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/claims/claim-highly-entangled-quantum-dynamics-is-the-most-natural-arena-for-meaningful-quant--3b6f2f5ba268.json ---\n{\n  \"added_at\": \"2026-04-26T15:52:46Z\",\n  \"claim_id\": \"claim-highly-entangled-quantum-dynamics-is-the-most-natural-arena-for-meaningful-quant--3b6f2f5ba268\",\n  \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n    \"The simulation of highly entangled quantum matter is the natural arena where quantum computers seem to have a clear advantage over classical ones.\",\n    \"Valuable insights might already be gleaned using noisy devices with of order 100 qubits.\"\n  ],\n  \"schema_version\": \"claim_record_v1\",\n  \"short_title\": \"Quantum dynamics is promising\",\n  \"source_id\": \"source-01-quantum-computing-in-the-nisq-era-and-beyond--2ce59b8ba8bb\",\n  \"text\": \"Highly entangled quantum dynamics is the most natural arena for meaningful quantum advantage, and noisy devices around one hundred qubits may already yield valuable physical insight.\"\n}\n/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/claims/claim-for-the-hardest-reported-random-circuit-sampling-task-sycamore-produced-one-mill--07db9778c4a3.json\n--- /Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/claims/claim-for-the-hardest-reported-random-circuit-sampling-task-sycamore-produced-one-mill--07db9778c4a3.json ---\n{\n  \"added_at\": \"2026-04-26T15:59:28Z\",\n  \"claim_id\": \"claim-for-the-hardest-reported-random-circuit-sampling-task-sycamore-produced-one-mill--07db9778c4a3\",\n  \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n    \"For m = 20, obtaining a million samples on the quantum processor takes 200 seconds, whereas an equal-fidelity classical sampling would take 10,000 years on a million cores.\"\n  ],\n  \"schema_version\": \"claim_record_v1\",\n  \"short_title\": \"Quantum sampling outpaces classical\",\n  \"source_id\": \"source-02-quantum-supremacy-using-a-programmable-superconducting-processor--f98990eb22d8\",\n  \"text\": \"For the hardest reported random-circuit sampling task, Sycamore produced one million samples in about 200 seconds while the authors estimated equal-fidelity classical sampling would take about 10,000 years on one million cores.\"\n}\n/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/claims/claim-the-sycamore-superconducting-processor-created-random-circuit-quantum-states-on--2b4eeabd4063.json\n--- /Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/claims/claim-the-sycamore-superconducting-processor-created-random-circuit-quantum-states-on--2b4eeabd4063.json ---\n{\n  \"added_at\": \"2026-04-26T15:59:28Z\",\n  \"claim_id\": \"claim-the-sycamore-superconducting-processor-created-random-circuit-quantum-states-on--2b4eeabd4063\",\n  \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n    \"Here we report the use of a processor with programmable superconducting qubits to create quantum states on 53 qubits, corresponding to a computational state-space of dimension 2^53 (about 10^16).\"\n  ],\n  \"schema_version\": \"claim_record_v1\",\n  \"short_title\": \"Sycamore reaches 53 qubits\",\n  \"source_id\": \"source-02-quantum-supremacy-using-a-programmable-superconducting-processor--f98990eb22d8\",\n  \"text\": \"The Sycamore superconducting processor created random-circuit quantum states on 53 qubits, corresponding to a computational state space of 2^53, about 10^16 basis states.\"\n}\n/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/claims/claim-quantum-computers-are-not-expected-to-efficiently-solve-hard-np-hard-combinatori--0d5f718c4d47.json\n--- /Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/claims/claim-quantum-computers-are-not-expected-to-efficiently-solve-hard-np-hard-combinatori--0d5f718c4d47.json ---\n{\n  \"added_at\": \"2026-04-26T15:52:46Z\",\n  \"claim_id\": \"claim-quantum-computers-are-not-expected-to-efficiently-solve-hard-np-hard-combinatori--0d5f718c4d47\",\n  \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n    \"We don\\u2019t expect, for example, that a quantum computer will be able to solve efficiently the hard instances of NP-hard problems like the traveling salesman problem.\",\n    \"Quantum computers can speed up exhaustive search, but only modestly, so NP-hard problems are likely to be quantumly hard as well as classically hard.\"\n  ],\n  \"schema_version\": \"claim_record_v1\",\n  \"short_title\": \"NP-hard problems remain hard\",\n  \"source_id\": \"source-01-quantum-computing-in-the-nisq-era-and-beyond--2ce59b8ba8bb\",\n  \"text\": \"Quantum computers are not expected to efficiently solve hard NP-hard combinatorial optimization instances, although they may provide modest search speedups or useful heuristic approximations.\"\n}\n/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/claims/claim-the-demonstrated-speedup-is-specific-to-random-circuit-sampling-and-does-not-rem--47d4f349751e.json\n--- /Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/claims/claim-the-demonstrated-speedup-is-specific-to-random-circuit-sampling-and-does-not-rem--47d4f349751e.json ---\n{\n  \"added_at\": \"2026-04-26T15:59:28Z\",\n  \"claim_id\": \"claim-the-demonstrated-speedup-is-specific-to-random-circuit-sampling-and-does-not-rem--47d4f349751e\",\n  \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n    \"Realizing the full promise of quantum computing using Shor's algorithm for factoring, for example, still requires technical leaps to engineer fault-tolerant logical qubits.\"\n  ],\n  \"schema_version\": \"claim_record_v1\",\n  \"short_title\": \"Fault tolerance remains necessary\",\n  \"source_id\": \"source-02-quantum-supremacy-using-a-programmable-superconducting-processor--f98990eb22d8\",\n  \"text\": \"The demonstrated speedup is specific to random circuit sampling and does not remove the need for fault-tolerant logical qubits for applications such as Shor-style factoring.\"\n}\n/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/claims/claim-noise-resilient-circuit-design-can-extend-nisq-computational-reach-but-it-may-tr--aed4bcfeaa39.json\n--- /Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/claims/claim-noise-resilient-circuit-design-can-extend-nisq-computational-reach-but-it-may-tr--aed4bcfeaa39.json ---\n{\n  \"added_at\": \"2026-04-26T15:52:46Z\",\n  \"claim_id\": \"claim-noise-resilient-circuit-design-can-extend-nisq-computational-reach-but-it-may-tr--aed4bcfeaa39\",\n  \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n    \"There is a substantial opportunity for experimentalists and theorists, working together over the next few years, to find better ways of making quantum circuits noise resilient.\",\n    \"Making a quantum circuit more noise resilient may also make it easier to simulate classically.\"\n  ],\n  \"schema_version\": \"claim_record_v1\",\n  \"short_title\": \"Noise resilience has tradeoffs\",\n  \"source_id\": \"source-01-quantum-computing-in-the-nisq-era-and-beyond--2ce59b8ba8bb\",\n  \"text\": \"Noise-resilient circuit design can extend NISQ computational reach, but it may trade off against classical simulability.\"\n}\n/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/claims/claim-the-largest-elided-53-qubit-20-cycle-circuits-yielded-f-xeb-2-24-0-21-10-3-over--0a24f12d0686.json\n--- /Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/claims/claim-the-largest-elided-53-qubit-20-cycle-circuits-yielded-f-xeb-2-24-0-21-10-3-over--0a24f12d0686.json ---\n{\n  \"added_at\": \"2026-04-26T15:59:28Z\",\n  \"claim_id\": \"claim-the-largest-elided-53-qubit-20-cycle-circuits-yielded-f-xeb-2-24-0-21-10-3-over--0a24f12d0686\",\n  \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n    \"For the largest circuit with 53 qubits and 20 cycles, we collected N_s = 30 \\u00d7 10^6 samples over ten circuit instances, obtaining F_XEB = (2.24 \\u00b10.21) \\u00d7 10^-3 for the elided circuits. With 5\\u03c3 confidence, we assert that the average fidelity of running these circuits on the quantum processor is greater than at least 0.1%.\"\n  ],\n  \"schema_version\": \"claim_record_v1\",\n  \"short_title\": \"Elided circuits retain fidelity\",\n  \"source_id\": \"source-02-quantum-supremacy-using-a-programmable-superconducting-processor--f98990eb22d8\",\n  \"text\": \"The largest elided 53-qubit, 20-cycle circuits yielded F_XEB = (2.24 \\u00b1 0.21) \\u00d7 10^-3 over 30 million samples, supporting an average fidelity above 0.1% with 5\\u03c3 confidence.\"\n}\n--- relations files ---\n/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/relations/rel-153583aa6609adfa8935fe692fb9c6ab3153ad83c507479a7e639a4639669d67.json\n--- /Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/relations/rel-153583aa6609adfa8935fe692fb9c6ab3153ad83c507479a7e639a4639669d67.json ---\n{\n  \"below_040_streak\": 0,\n  \"contradiction_confidence_band\": \"unknown\",\n  \"dst_claim_id\": \"claim-nisq-computers-with-roughly-50-to-a-few-hundred-qubits-mark-a-classically-hard-s--2d5ec194bedf\",\n  \"dst_claim_ref\": \"0\",\n  \"last_evaluated_run_id\": null,\n  \"rationale\": \"Noise-resilient design is presented as a way to stretch, but not remove, the noise-imposed limits of NISQ devices.\",\n  \"relation_file_id\": \"rel-153583aa6609adfa8935fe692fb9c6ab3153ad83c507479a7e639a4639669d67\",\n  \"relation_id\": \"supports:claim-noise-resilient-circuit-design-can-extend-nisq-computational-reach-but-it-may-tr--aed4bcfeaa39->claim-nisq-computers-with-roughly-50-to-a-few-hundred-qubits-mark-a-classically-hard-s--2d5ec194bedf\",\n  \"relation_type\": \"supports\",\n  \"src_claim_id\": \"claim-noise-resilient-circuit-design-can-extend-nisq-computational-reach-but-it-may-tr--aed4bcfeaa39\",\n  \"src_claim_ref\": \"8\",\n  \"status\": \"open\"\n}\n/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/relations/rel-1af95dc9d41e2d00a9981074719e9e5dee9ac3e88f78cd7eac4682e6f40c579e.json\n--- /Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/relations/rel-1af95dc9d41e2d00a9981074719e9e5dee9ac3e88f78cd7eac4682e6f40c579e.json ---\n{\n  \"below_040_streak\": 0,\n  \"contradiction_confidence_band\": \"unknown\",\n  \"dst_claim_id\": \"claim-for-the-hardest-reported-random-circuit-sampling-task-sycamore-produced-one-mill--07db9778c4a3\",\n  \"dst_claim_ref\": \"1\",\n  \"last_evaluated_run_id\": null,\n  \"rationale\": \"The exponential classical scaling of random circuits explains why the reported runtime gap grows at the largest width and depth.\",\n  \"relation_file_id\": \"rel-1af95dc9d41e2d00a9981074719e9e5dee9ac3e88f78cd7eac4682e6f40c579e\",\n  \"relation_id\": \"supports:claim-random-quantum-circuits-become-exponentially-harder-to-simulate-classically-as-q--f7cd81f80f31->claim-for-the-hardest-reported-random-circuit-sampling-task-sycamore-produced-one-mill--07db9778c4a3\",\n  \"relation_type\": \"supports\",\n  \"src_claim_id\": \"claim-random-quantum-circuits-become-exponentially-harder-to-simulate-classically-as-q--f7cd81f80f31\",\n  \"src_claim_ref\": \"6\",\n  \"status\": \"open\"\n}\n/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/relations/rel-93cd412d7857c494ba3e940992fdc77e6b4cb0db92e110a59d8c17a56a0dcbfc.json\n--- /Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/relations/rel-93cd412d7857c494ba3e940992fdc77e6b4cb0db92e110a59d8c17a56a0dcbfc.json ---\n{\n  \"below_040_streak\": 0,\n  \"contradiction_confidence_band\": \"unknown\",\n  \"dst_claim_id\": \"claim-quantum-computers-are-not-expected-to-efficiently-solve-hard-np-hard-combinatori--0d5f718c4d47\",\n  \"dst_claim_ref\": \"6\",\n  \"last_evaluated_run_id\": null,\n  \"rationale\": \"Both claims temper optimization expectations by emphasizing hard classical baselines and unresolved quantum advantage.\",\n  \"relation_file_id\": \"rel-93cd412d7857c494ba3e940992fdc77e6b4cb0db92e110a59d8c17a56a0dcbfc\",\n  \"relation_id\": \"similar:claim-hybrid-qaoa-and-vqe-approaches-are-plausible-nisq-era-testbeds-but-their-ability--31ec67369767|claim-quantum-computers-are-not-expected-to-efficiently-solve-hard-np-hard-combinatori--0d5f718c4d47\",\n  \"relation_type\": \"similar\",\n  \"src_claim_id\": \"claim-hybrid-qaoa-and-vqe-approaches-are-plausible-nisq-era-testbeds-but-their-ability--31ec67369767\",\n  \"src_claim_ref\": \"5\",\n  \"status\": \"open\"\n}\n/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/relations/rel-c898e800c6ae005c4eb3a825a6266c29b754b43205d79a5fb6daeb4b6d06b4fa.json\n--- /Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/relations/rel-c898e800c6ae005c4eb3a825a6266c29b754b43205d79a5fb6daeb4b6d06b4fa.json ---\n{\n  \"below_040_streak\": 0,\n  \"contradiction_confidence_band\": \"unknown\",\n  \"dst_claim_id\": \"claim-nisq-computers-with-roughly-50-to-a-few-hundred-qubits-mark-a-classically-hard-s--2d5ec194bedf\",\n  \"dst_claim_ref\": \"0\",\n  \"last_evaluated_run_id\": null,\n  \"rationale\": \"The two-qubit error and thousand-gate estimates explain why NISQ scale does not by itself produce deep reliable computation.\",\n  \"relation_file_id\": \"rel-c898e800c6ae005c4eb3a825a6266c29b754b43205d79a5fb6daeb4b6d06b4fa\",\n  \"relation_id\": \"supports:claim-current-two-qubit-gate-and-measurement-error-rates-imply-that-uncorrected-nisq-c--68f47af68b82->claim-nisq-computers-with-roughly-50-to-a-few-hundred-qubits-mark-a-classically-hard-s--2d5ec194bedf\",\n  \"relation_type\": \"supports\",\n  \"src_claim_id\": \"claim-current-two-qubit-gate-and-measurement-error-rates-imply-that-uncorrected-nisq-c--68f47af68b82\",\n  \"src_claim_ref\": \"2\",\n  \"status\": \"open\"\n}\n/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/relations/rel-04809370c2554a7ce66e03f99322178b11278091075f3a31292d80432a35612a.json\n--- /Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/relations/rel-04809370c2554a7ce66e03f99322178b11278091075f3a31292d80432a35612a.json ---\n{\n  \"below_040_streak\": 0,\n  \"contradiction_confidence_band\": \"unknown\",\n  \"dst_claim_id\": \"claim-the-demonstrated-speedup-is-specific-to-random-circuit-sampling-and-does-not-rem--47d4f349751e\",\n  \"dst_claim_ref\": \"1\",\n  \"last_evaluated_run_id\": null,\n  \"rationale\": \"Both claims delimit the demonstration as a task-specific milestone rather than a general-purpose fault-tolerant quantum computer.\",\n  \"relation_file_id\": \"rel-04809370c2554a7ce66e03f99322178b11278091075f3a31292d80432a35612a\",\n  \"relation_id\": \"similar:claim-for-the-hardest-reported-random-circuit-sampling-task-sycamore-produced-one-mill--07db9778c4a3|claim-the-demonstrated-speedup-is-specific-to-random-circuit-sampling-and-does-not-rem--47d4f349751e\",\n  \"relation_type\": \"similar\",\n  \"src_claim_id\": \"claim-for-the-hardest-reported-random-circuit-sampling-task-sycamore-produced-one-mill--07db9778c4a3\",\n  \"src_claim_ref\": \"8\",\n  \"status\": \"open\"\n}\n/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/relations/rel-e807c7ca70656a359ae0a809aeecdb332c3329aa745730875ccc40ea6ff5d76f.json\n--- /Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/relations/rel-e807c7ca70656a359ae0a809aeecdb332c3329aa745730875ccc40ea6ff5d76f.json ---\n{\n  \"below_040_streak\": 0,\n  \"contradiction_confidence_band\": \"unknown\",\n  \"dst_claim_id\": \"claim-scalable-fault-tolerant-quantum-computing-depends-on-quantum-error-correction-bu--cf48bc246343\",\n  \"dst_claim_ref\": \"3\",\n  \"last_evaluated_run_id\": null,\n  \"rationale\": \"Lower gate error rates reduce both near-term circuit failure and the future overhead required for fault-tolerant operation.\",\n  \"relation_file_id\": \"rel-e807c7ca70656a359ae0a809aeecdb332c3329aa745730875ccc40ea6ff5d76f\",\n  \"relation_id\": \"supports:claim-lower-gate-error-rates-matter-both-because-they-allow-larger-uncorrected-nisq-ci--043e58605f51->claim-scalable-fault-tolerant-quantum-computing-depends-on-quantum-error-correction-bu--cf48bc246343\",\n  \"relation_type\": \"supports\",\n  \"src_claim_id\": \"claim-lower-gate-error-rates-matter-both-because-they-allow-larger-uncorrected-nisq-ci--043e58605f51\",\n  \"src_claim_ref\": \"9\",\n  \"status\": \"open\"\n}\n/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/relations/rel-3829cfadc2de1fbd047e4c1cb4df2934c3cc391dd753242cd1bd190b631d03d1.json\n--- /Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/relations/rel-3829cfadc2de1fbd047e4c1cb4df2934c3cc391dd753242cd1bd190b631d03d1.json ---\n{\n  \"below_040_streak\": 0,\n  \"contradiction_confidence_band\": \"unknown\",\n  \"dst_claim_id\": \"claim-quantum-computers-have-credible-grounds-for-super-classical-power-because-of-kno--2f85bd581bbb\",\n  \"dst_claim_ref\": \"4\",\n  \"last_evaluated_run_id\": null,\n  \"rationale\": \"The special promise of quantum dynamics follows from the broader argument that highly entangled systems resist classical simulation.\",\n  \"relation_file_id\": \"rel-3829cfadc2de1fbd047e4c1cb4df2934c3cc391dd753242cd1bd190b631d03d1\",\n  \"relation_id\": \"derived_from:claim-highly-entangled-quantum-dynamics-is-the-most-natural-arena-for-meaningful-quant--3b6f2f5ba268->claim-quantum-computers-have-credible-grounds-for-super-classical-power-because-of-kno--2f85bd581bbb\",\n  \"relation_type\": \"derived_from\",\n  \"src_claim_id\": \"claim-highly-entangled-quantum-dynamics-is-the-most-natural-arena-for-meaningful-quant--3b6f2f5ba268\",\n  \"src_claim_ref\": \"7\",\n  \"status\": \"open\"\n}\n/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/relations/rel-c07835453bdef1546efeb54528ba04f62712788363361fa9e290d5875ab9d501.json\n--- /Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/relations/rel-c07835453bdef1546efeb54528ba04f62712788363361fa9e290d5875ab9d501.json ---\n{\n  \"below_040_streak\": 0,\n  \"contradiction_confi