{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"set -euo pipefail\nout='/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/runs/run-20260426T160450Z--7gd5v9f70v/semantic/ingest_extraction.json'\nmkdir -p \\\"\"'$(dirname \"$out\")\"\npython3 - <<'\"'PY' > \\\"\"'$out\"\nimport json\nobj = {\n  \"source_date_inference\": {\n    \"confidence\": \"unknown\",\n    \"date\": None,\n    \"origin\": \"unknown\",\n    \"rationale\": \"The source record did not resolve a publication date. The markdown includes an arXiv stamp of 25 Jun 2008 and a separate generated-looking Dated line, so no normalized publication date is asserted.\"\n  },\n  \"source\": {\n    \"source_id\": \"source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\",\n    \"display_title\": \"Quantum Interconnects for the Quantum Internet\",\n    \"original_title\": \"The Quantum Internet\",\n    \"authors\": [\"H. J. Kimble\"],\n    \"source_locator\": \"03-the-quantum-internet.pdf\"\n  },\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Large quantum networks require reversible interconnects that convert quantum states between matter and light while preserving coherence and entanglement.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Networks require quantum interconnects\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"Fundamental to this endeavor are quantum interconnects that convert quantum states from one physical system to those of another in a reversible fashion.\",\n        \"Desiderata for a quantum interface include user-controlled coupling, robustness against imperfections, efficient error detection, and the rate hierarchy χ >> κ >> γ.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Quantum connectivity gives linked nodes a state space that scales exponentially across all nodes, unlike classically connected quantum processors whose state spaces remain separate by node.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Quantum channels expand state space\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"A network of quantum nodes linked by classical channels comprised of k nodes each with n qubits has a state space of dimension k2n, whereas a fully quantum network linked by quantum channels has an exponentially larger dimension 2kn.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Strong cavity-QED coupling supplies a physical regime in which individual atoms and photons can interact coherently enough to function as quantum-network nodes and interfaces.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Strong coupling enables interfaces\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"Experiments in cavity QED explore strong coupling with g > (γ, κ), where γ is the atomic decay rate to modes other than the cavity mode and κ is the decay rate of the cavity mode itself.\",\n        \"The number of photons required to saturate the intracavity atom is n0 ∼ γ²/g², and the number of atoms required to affect the intracavity field is N0 ∼ κγ/g²; strong coupling explores the regime (n0, N0) << 1.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Dark-state adiabatic control can reversibly map a single-photon optical field into a trapped atom and later map the stored atomic state back into a flying optical field.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Dark states map photons reversibly\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"The dark state is |D⟩ = cos θ |a⟩|0⟩ + sin θ |b⟩|1⟩, with the control field Ω(t) steering the mapping between atomic and photonic components.\",\n        \"In the ideal case, |φfield(t)⟩|b⟩ → |0⟩(c1|a⟩ + c0|b⟩) ... → |φfield(t + τ)⟩|b⟩.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Controlled cavity pulses can generate entanglement first between an atom and an emitted photon and then between sequentially emitted photon polarizations.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Cavity pulses entangle photons\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"An initial control field Ω1(t1) produces entanglement between internal atomic states and the polarization state of a flying photon; a second control field Ω2(t2) returns the atom to its initial state while generating a second flying photon, thereby leading to entanglement between the two emitted fields.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"The DLCZ protocol creates heralded entanglement between atomic ensembles by interfering emitted single-photon fields and conditioning on a detector event.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"DLCZ heralds ensemble entanglement\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"A photoelectric detection event at either detector projects the ensembles into |ΨL,R⟩ = 1/√2 [|0a⟩L|1a⟩R ± eiη1|1a⟩L|0a⟩R].\",\n        \"Detection of a photon from one or the other atomic ensemble in an indistinguishable fashion results in an entangled state with one collective spin excitation shared coherently between the ensembles.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Atomic-ensemble experiments had achieved stored heralded entanglement, high retrieval efficiencies, and minimal scalable-node functionality by the time of the source discussion.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Ensemble experiments store entanglement\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"Conditional readout efficiencies of 74% in free space and 84% in a cavity were realized for state transfer from a single collective spin excitation to a single photon.\",\n        \"Heralded entanglement between distant ensembles was achieved in 2005, with the entangled state stored in quantum memories located in distinct apparatuses separated by 3 m; later work inferred C = 0.9 ± 0.3 for the concurrence.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Electromagnetically induced transparency enables deterministic reversible mapping of photonic states and photonic entanglement into and out of atomic-ensemble quantum memories.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"EIT maps photonic memories\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"Deterministic mapping of quantum states of light to and from atomic ensembles is possible by way of electromagnetically induced transparency.\",\n        \"Reversible mapping of photonic entanglement into and out of pairs of quantum memories was realized by an EIT process.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Full density-matrix characterization becomes an impractical verification strategy as quantum networks grow because the state description scales exponentially with network size.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Verification scales poorly\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"Attempting to determine the density matrix ρ(t) for the network surely fails because of the exponential growth in ρ(t) with the size of the network.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Scalable quantum networks are likely to be heterogeneous systems that combine cavity-QED nodes, ensemble memories, repeaters, local processing, error-corrected teleportation, and stronger entanglement-verification procedures.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Heterogeneous networks need verification\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"The current state of the art is primitive relative to that required for robust and scalable implementation of sophisticated network protocols; quantum memories, local quantum processing, quantum repeaters, and error-corrected teleportation remain ambitious goals.\",\n        \"Quantum networks will evolve as heterogeneous entities, and a critical task is the development of unambiguous procedures for entanglement verification.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_items\": [\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"evidence-quantum-channel-state-space--f3f60c660749\",\n      \"title\": \"Quantum Channel State-Space Scaling\",\n      \"excerpt\": \"A network of quantum nodes linked by classical channels comprised of k nodes each with n qubits has a state space of dimension k2n, whereas a fully quantum network linked by quantum channels has an exponentially larger dimension 2kn.\",\n      \"overview\": \"This scaling argument supplies the source'\"'s central motivation for true quantum connectivity. It is not a measurement but a formal comparison between separated node Hilbert spaces and a fully connected quantum network, showing why inter-node quantum channels can matter even apart from any particular algorithm.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"formal_argument\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"1\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Introduction\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-interface-rate-hierarchy--7240c022b1bb\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Interface Rate Hierarchy\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"Desiderata for a quantum interface include that χ(t) should be user-controlled for clocking states to and from the quantum memory, that the physical processes should be robust in the face of imperfections, and that the bandwidth rate κ should be large compared to parasitic losses γ while both are small compared to coherent coupling, χ >> κ >> γ.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"The rate hierarchy states the operating condition the source treats as necessary for useful light-matter interfaces. It connects abstract network goals to a concrete physical design constraint: coherent coupling must dominate useful input-output bandwidth, and parasitic loss must be smaller still.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"model_constraint\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"0\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Fig. 1(b)\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-cavity-qed-coupling--cd7f6b2f11e2\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Cavity-QED Coupling Conditions\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"The coupling frequency for one atom to a single resonator mode is g = sqrt(|ε·μ0|²ωC/(2ℏε0Vm)). Experiments in cavity QED explore strong coupling with g > (γ, κ); the saturation photon number is n0 ∼ γ²/g² and the critical atom number is N0 ∼ κγ/g², so strong coupling explores (n0, N0) << 1.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"These equations define the physical threshold behind the cavity-QED route to network nodes. They show why small mode volume, high quality factor, and low loss are not incidental engineering details but determine whether single photons and single atoms can coherently control one another.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"equation\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"2\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Eq. 1\\\", \\\"Fig. 2\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-dark-state-memory-map--08064195a001\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Dark-State Memory Mapping\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"The atom-cavity interaction has a dark state |D⟩ = cos θ |a⟩|0⟩ + sin θ |b⟩|1⟩. In the ideal transfer sequence, |φfield(t)⟩|b⟩ → |0⟩(c1|a⟩ + c0|b⟩) ... → |φfield(t + τ)⟩|b⟩, with Ω1(t) and Ω2(t + τ) controlling absorption into memory and later emission back to the field.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"The dark-state construction is the clearest concrete mechanism for reversible light-matter conversion in the source. It explains how a photon can be absorbed into a long-lived atomic superposition and re-emitted later while avoiding population of the lossy excited state in the ideal adiabatic limit.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"proof_step\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"3\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Box 1\\\", \\\"Eq. 1\\\", \\\"Eq. 2\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-dlcz-ensemble-bell-state--08064195a002\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"DLCZ Heralded Ensemble State\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"In the ideal case and to lowest order in p, a photoelectric detection event at either detector projects the ensembles into |ΨL,R⟩ = 1/√2 [|0a⟩L|1a⟩R ± eiη1|1a⟩L|0a⟩R], with the sign set by whether D1 or D2 records the event and η1 determined by the channel phase difference.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This equation is the source's formal core for measurement-induced ensemble entanglement. The detector click erases which-ensemble information, leaving one collective excitation coherently shared between remote ensembles; the phase-stability condition marks a practical limit on the idealized expression.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"equation\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"5\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Eq. 2\\\", \\\"Fig. 3(a)\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-ensemble-concurrence-readout--08064195a003\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Stored Ensemble Entanglement Metrics\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"Conditional readout efficiencies of 74% in free space and 84% in a cavity were realized for transfer from a stored collective spin excitation to a single photon. Heralded entanglement was stored in ensembles in distinct apparatuses separated by 3 m, and later work inferred concurrence C = 0.9 ± 0.3 for entanglement stored between L and R ensembles.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"These measurements show that the DLCZ-style architecture was more than a paper protocol: the necessary pieces of retrieval, remote storage, and entanglement quantification had laboratory demonstrations. The values do not establish a full repeater, but they ground the claim that primitive scalable-node functions were emerging.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"measurement\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"6\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Fig. 3(b)\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-microtoroid-coupling-metrics--08064195a004\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Microtoroid Coupling Metrics\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"For silica microtoroidal resonators, projected critical photon and atom numbers were (n0, N0) ≃ (2×10−5, 10−6). Quality factors Q = 4×10\"'^8 at λ = 1550 nm and Q ≃ 10''^8 at λ = 850 nm had been realized, with prospects for Q ∼ 10''^10, and coupling efficiency into and out of the resonator could approach 0.99–0.999 while remaining in strong coupling.\",\n      \"overview\": \"The microtoroid data identify one candidate path beyond conventional Fabry-Perot cavities. The metrics matter because large-scale networks need many integrated nodes with high input-output efficiency; the numbers also remain partly projected, so they support feasibility rather than completed network operation.\",\n      \"evidence_type\": \"model_constraint\",\n      \"claim_refs\": [\"2\", \"9\"],\n      \"source_id\": \"source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\",\n      \"page_refs\": [\"Box 2\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"evidence-eit-memory-transfer--08064195a006\",\n      \"title\": \"EIT Memory Transfer Results\",\n      \"excerpt\": \"Deterministic mapping of quantum states of light to and from atomic ensembles is possible by way of electromagnetically induced transparency. The cited work demonstrated storage and retrieval of classical pulses, extended the process to single photons, achieved entanglement between two ensembles coupled to a cavity mode, and realized reversible mapping of photonic entanglement into and out of pairs of quantum memories.\",\n      \"overview\": \"This evidence establishes the source'\"'s second route to reversible interfaces: ensemble memories using EIT rather than single atoms in cavities. It gathers a progression from classical pulse storage to single-photon and entanglement transfer, while relying on cited experimental achievements rather than a single new measurement in this review-like source.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"observational_result\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"7\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Atomic ensembles section\\\", \\\"Fig. 1(d)\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-density-matrix-growth--08064195a005\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Density-Matrix Verification Barrier\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"As moderately complex quantum networks are realized, it will become difficult to assess whether entanglement extends across the whole network. Attempting to determine the density matrix ρ(t) for the network surely fails because of the exponential growth in ρ(t) with the size of the network.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"The verification argument clarifies a limit that is independent of any one hardware platform. Even if nodes and interfaces work locally, conventional full-state reconstruction does not scale, so practical quantum networks require more targeted diagnostics or functional tests of multipartite entanglement.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"formal_argument\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"8\\\", \\\"9\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Extending entanglement section\\\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"relations\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"supports\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"2\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"0\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Strong cavity-QED coupling is one physical mechanism for the reversible light-matter interconnects required by networks.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"derived_from\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"3\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"0\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"The dark-state mapping protocol instantiates the general reversible-interconnect requirement.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"supports\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"5\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"0\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"DLCZ ensemble entanglement supplies a second grounded route for distributing coherence between network nodes.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"supports\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"6\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"5\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"The stored-entanglement experiments demonstrate key laboratory capabilities presupposed by the DLCZ protocol.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"similar\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"7\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"3\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Both claims concern reversible mapping between optical fields and matter memories, using different physical platforms.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"supports\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"8\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"9\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"The scaling barrier for verification motivates stronger procedures for heterogeneous scalable networks.\\\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"summary\\\": \\\"H. J. Kimble's The Quantum Internet frames quantum networks as systems of nodes and channels that require reversible light-matter interfaces, strong single-photon nonlinearities, ensemble memories, repeaters, and scalable verification. The source surveys cavity-QED and atomic-ensemble routes, emphasizing both experimental progress and the difficulty of extending and certifying entanglement in larger heterogeneous networks.\\\",\n  \\\"warnings\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"code\\\": \\\"missing_publication_date\\\",\n      \\\"message\\\": \\\"Publication date could not be resolved from the source record; date remains null.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"code\\\": \\\"date_signal_conflict\\\",\n      \\\"message\\\": \\\"Markdown contains an arXiv stamp of 25 Jun 2008 and a separate Dated line reading November 26, 2024, so the output does not infer a normalized source date.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"code\\\": \\\"review_like_source\\\",\n      \\\"message\\\": \\\"Several evidence items summarize formal arguments or cited experimental results from a review-style source rather than new measurements introduced solely by this document.\\\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"source_dossier\\\": {\n    \\\"summary_short\\\": \\\"This source presents the quantum internet as a network architecture built from quantum nodes, quantum channels, and reversible light-matter interfaces. It argues that genuine quantum connectivity provides scaling advantages over classically linked quantum devices and reviews two main physical routes: cavity QED with single atoms and atomic ensembles using DLCZ/EIT protocols. The piece is also cautious: memories, repeaters, local processing, error-corrected teleportation, and scalable verification remain major unsolved requirements.\\\",\n    \\\"summary_long\\\": \\\"Kimble's source treats the quantum internet not as a near-term communication product but as a research program for connecting quantum systems without destroying their coherence. The central architectural idea is that quantum information must be generated, processed, and stored locally in nodes, then moved or entangled across channels by interfaces that reversibly translate states between matter and light. This distinguishes a fully quantum network from merely classically coordinated quantum processors: when channels preserve quantum coherence, the accessible state space scales across all nodes rather than remaining a sum of separate node spaces. The physical burden then falls on light-matter interfaces. In cavity QED, single atoms inside high-finesse resonators can reach strong coupling, allowing a single photon and a single atom to interact coherently; dark-state adiabatic protocols show how a photonic qubit can be absorbed into an atomic memory and later re-emitted. The source pairs that route with atomic-ensemble schemes, especially DLCZ, where weak write pulses, single-photon interference, and heralding create a shared collective spin excitation between distant ensembles. It cites experimental progress in retrieval efficiency, stored entanglement over meter-scale separation, Bell-inequality tests, entanglement swapping, and EIT-based reversible memory mapping. At the same time, the paper's practical message is restrained. The demonstrated pieces are primitive relative to full repeater-based, error-corrected networks, and complex networks create a verification problem because full density-matrix reconstruction scales exponentially. The mature quantum internet implied here is therefore heterogeneous: it will likely combine atom-cavity systems, ensemble memories, integrated resonators, quantum repeaters, local processing, and new entanglement diagnostics rather than rely on a single uniform device platform.\\\",\n    \\\"sections\\\": [\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Network Motivation\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The source's first move is architectural: quantum nodes linked only by classical channels do not realize the same state space as nodes joined by quantum channels. This matters because networked quantum processors cannot simply exchange measurement records and retain the same computational or metrological possibilities; they need channels that carry quantum states or distribute entanglement with high fidelity.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"0\\\", \\\"1\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Light-Matter Interface Requirements\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The proposed network depends on reversible conversion between stationary matter memories and flying photons. Kimble makes the requirement concrete through controllable coupling χ(t), useful bandwidth κ, parasitic loss γ, robustness to imperfections, and error handling. The interface is therefore both a physics problem and a systems problem: memory timing, transfer fidelity, and loss budgets all matter.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"0\\\", \\\"2\\\", \\\"3\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Cavity-QED Route\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"Cavity QED supplies one candidate hardware path by enhancing the electric field of a single photon in a small, high-Q resonator. In the strong-coupling regime, g dominates γ and κ, and the critical photon and atom numbers fall below unity. Dark-state protocols then use shaped control pulses to absorb and re-emit photonic states while avoiding lossy excited-state population in the ideal limit.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"2\\\", \\\"3\\\", \\\"4\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Atomic-Ensemble Route\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The ensemble approach replaces single trapped atoms with collective excitations shared over many atoms. DLCZ uses weak write pulses and indistinguishable single-photon detection to herald a remote entangled spin excitation, while EIT provides a deterministic memory mechanism for mapping optical states into and out of ensembles. These routes trade deterministic local control for collective enhancement and heralded operation.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"5\\\", \\\"7\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Experimental Evidence\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The source grounds its optimism in reported milestones: efficient readout from stored collective excitations, heralded entanglement between ensembles separated by 3 m, inferred concurrence of 0.9 ± 0.3, Bell-inequality violation after memory readout, and early entanglement swapping. These are not full quantum repeaters, but they demonstrate several primitives needed for scalable nodes.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"6\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Scaling Limits\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"Kimble emphasizes that making larger networks is not just a matter of adding more working links. Verification itself becomes hard because full density-matrix reconstruction grows exponentially with network size, while purely functional algorithm tests may lack diagnostic value when networks fail. This motivates physical, scalable, and task-relevant tests of multipartite entanglement.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"8\\\", \\\"9\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Practical Interpretation\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The paper's practical stance is ambitious but not triumphalist. It expects heterogeneous networks that combine cavity nodes, ensemble memories, integrated resonators, repeaters, and error-corrected teleportation. The near-term value is in assembling and validating primitives; the long-term value is a platform for quantum communication, distributed processing, metrology, and controlled many-body quantum dynamics.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"0\\\", \\\"2\\\", \\\"5\\\", \\\"9\\\"]\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\nprint(json.dumps(obj, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"set -euo pipefail\nout='/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/runs/run-20260426T160450Z--7gd5v9f70v/semantic/ingest_extraction.json'\nmkdir -p \\\"\"'$(dirname \"$out\")\"\npython3 - <<'\"'PY' > \\\"\"'$out\"\nimport json\nobj = {\n  \"source_date_inference\": {\n    \"confidence\": \"unknown\",\n    \"date\": None,\n    \"origin\": \"unknown\",\n    \"rationale\": \"The source record did not resolve a publication date. The markdown includes an arXiv stamp of 25 Jun 2008 and a separate generated-looking Dated line, so no normalized publication date is asserted.\"\n  },\n  \"source\": {\n    \"source_id\": \"source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\",\n    \"display_title\": \"Quantum Interconnects for the Quantum Internet\",\n    \"original_title\": \"The Quantum Internet\",\n    \"authors\": [\"H. J. Kimble\"],\n    \"source_locator\": \"03-the-quantum-internet.pdf\"\n  },\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Large quantum networks require reversible interconnects that convert quantum states between matter and light while preserving coherence and entanglement.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Networks require quantum interconnects\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"Fundamental to this endeavor are quantum interconnects that convert quantum states from one physical system to those of another in a reversible fashion.\",\n        \"Desiderata for a quantum interface include user-controlled coupling, robustness against imperfections, efficient error detection, and the rate hierarchy χ >> κ >> γ.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Quantum connectivity gives linked nodes a state space that scales exponentially across all nodes, unlike classically connected quantum processors whose state spaces remain separate by node.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Quantum channels expand state space\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"A network of quantum nodes linked by classical channels comprised of k nodes each with n qubits has a state space of dimension k2n, whereas a fully quantum network linked by quantum channels has an exponentially larger dimension 2kn.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Strong cavity-QED coupling supplies a physical regime in which individual atoms and photons can interact coherently enough to function as quantum-network nodes and interfaces.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Strong coupling enables interfaces\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"Experiments in cavity QED explore strong coupling with g > (γ, κ), where γ is the atomic decay rate to modes other than the cavity mode and κ is the decay rate of the cavity mode itself.\",\n        \"The number of photons required to saturate the intracavity atom is n0 ∼ γ²/g², and the number of atoms required to affect the intracavity field is N0 ∼ κγ/g²; strong coupling explores the regime (n0, N0) << 1.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Dark-state adiabatic control can reversibly map a single-photon optical field into a trapped atom and later map the stored atomic state back into a flying optical field.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Dark states map photons reversibly\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"The dark state is |D⟩ = cos θ |a⟩|0⟩ + sin θ |b⟩|1⟩, with the control field Ω(t) steering the mapping between atomic and photonic components.\",\n        \"In the ideal case, |φfield(t)⟩|b⟩ → |0⟩(c1|a⟩ + c0|b⟩) ... → |φfield(t + τ)⟩|b⟩.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Controlled cavity pulses can generate entanglement first between an atom and an emitted photon and then between sequentially emitted photon polarizations.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Cavity pulses entangle photons\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"An initial control field Ω1(t1) produces entanglement between internal atomic states and the polarization state of a flying photon; a second control field Ω2(t2) returns the atom to its initial state while generating a second flying photon, thereby leading to entanglement between the two emitted fields.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"The DLCZ protocol creates heralded entanglement between atomic ensembles by interfering emitted single-photon fields and conditioning on a detector event.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"DLCZ heralds ensemble entanglement\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"A photoelectric detection event at either detector projects the ensembles into |ΨL,R⟩ = 1/√2 [|0a⟩L|1a⟩R ± eiη1|1a⟩L|0a⟩R].\",\n        \"Detection of a photon from one or the other atomic ensemble in an indistinguishable fashion results in an entangled state with one collective spin excitation shared coherently between the ensembles.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Atomic-ensemble experiments had achieved stored heralded entanglement, high retrieval efficiencies, and minimal scalable-node functionality by the time of the source discussion.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Ensemble experiments store entanglement\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"Conditional readout efficiencies of 74% in free space and 84% in a cavity were realized for state transfer from a single collective spin excitation to a single photon.\",\n        \"Heralded entanglement between distant ensembles was achieved in 2005, with the entangled state stored in quantum memories located in distinct apparatuses separated by 3 m; later work inferred C = 0.9 ± 0.3 for the concurrence.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Electromagnetically induced transparency enables deterministic reversible mapping of photonic states and photonic entanglement into and out of atomic-ensemble quantum memories.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"EIT maps photonic memories\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"Deterministic mapping of quantum states of light to and from atomic ensembles is possible by way of electromagnetically induced transparency.\",\n        \"Reversible mapping of photonic entanglement into and out of pairs of quantum memories was realized by an EIT process.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Full density-matrix characterization becomes an impractical verification strategy as quantum networks grow because the state description scales exponentially with network size.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Verification scales poorly\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"Attempting to determine the density matrix ρ(t) for the network surely fails because of the exponential growth in ρ(t) with the size of the network.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Scalable quantum networks are likely to be heterogeneous systems that combine cavity-QED nodes, ensemble memories, repeaters, local processing, error-corrected teleportation, and stronger entanglement-verification procedures.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Heterogeneous networks need verification\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"The current state of the art is primitive relative to that required for robust and scalable implementation of sophisticated network protocols; quantum memories, local quantum processing, quantum repeaters, and error-corrected teleportation remain ambitious goals.\",\n        \"Quantum networks will evolve as heterogeneous entities, and a critical task is the development of unambiguous procedures for entanglement verification.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_items\": [\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"evidence-quantum-channel-state-space--f3f60c660749\",\n      \"title\": \"Quantum Channel State-Space Scaling\",\n      \"excerpt\": \"A network of quantum nodes linked by classical channels comprised of k nodes each with n qubits has a state space of dimension k2n, whereas a fully quantum network linked by quantum channels has an exponentially larger dimension 2kn.\",\n      \"overview\": \"This scaling argument supplies the source'\"'s central motivation for true quantum connectivity. It is not a measurement but a formal comparison between separated node Hilbert spaces and a fully connected quantum network, showing why inter-node quantum channels can matter even apart from any particular algorithm.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"formal_argument\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"1\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Introduction\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-interface-rate-hierarchy--7240c022b1bb\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Interface Rate Hierarchy\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"Desiderata for a quantum interface include that χ(t) should be user-controlled for clocking states to and from the quantum memory, that the physical processes should be robust in the face of imperfections, and that the bandwidth rate κ should be large compared to parasitic losses γ while both are small compared to coherent coupling, χ >> κ >> γ.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"The rate hierarchy states the operating condition the source treats as necessary for useful light-matter interfaces. It connects abstract network goals to a concrete physical design constraint: coherent coupling must dominate useful input-output bandwidth, and parasitic loss must be smaller still.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"model_constraint\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"0\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Fig. 1(b)\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-cavity-qed-coupling--cd7f6b2f11e2\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Cavity-QED Coupling Conditions\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"The coupling frequency for one atom to a single resonator mode is g = sqrt(|ε·μ0|²ωC/(2ℏε0Vm)). Experiments in cavity QED explore strong coupling with g > (γ, κ); the saturation photon number is n0 ∼ γ²/g² and the critical atom number is N0 ∼ κγ/g², so strong coupling explores (n0, N0) << 1.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"These equations define the physical threshold behind the cavity-QED route to network nodes. They show why small mode volume, high quality factor, and low loss are not incidental engineering details but determine whether single photons and single atoms can coherently control one another.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"equation\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"2\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Eq. 1\\\", \\\"Fig. 2\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-dark-state-memory-map--08064195a001\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Dark-State Memory Mapping\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"The atom-cavity interaction has a dark state |D⟩ = cos θ |a⟩|0⟩ + sin θ |b⟩|1⟩. In the ideal transfer sequence, |φfield(t)⟩|b⟩ → |0⟩(c1|a⟩ + c0|b⟩) ... → |φfield(t + τ)⟩|b⟩, with Ω1(t) and Ω2(t + τ) controlling absorption into memory and later emission back to the field.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"The dark-state construction is the clearest concrete mechanism for reversible light-matter conversion in the source. It explains how a photon can be absorbed into a long-lived atomic superposition and re-emitted later while avoiding population of the lossy excited state in the ideal adiabatic limit.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"proof_step\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"3\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Box 1\\\", \\\"Eq. 1\\\", \\\"Eq. 2\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-dlcz-ensemble-bell-state--08064195a002\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"DLCZ Heralded Ensemble State\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"In the ideal case and to lowest order in p, a photoelectric detection event at either detector projects the ensembles into |ΨL,R⟩ = 1/√2 [|0a⟩L|1a⟩R ± eiη1|1a⟩L|0a⟩R], with the sign set by whether D1 or D2 records the event and η1 determined by the channel phase difference.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This equation is the source's formal core for measurement-induced ensemble entanglement. The detector click erases which-ensemble information, leaving one collective excitation coherently shared between remote ensembles; the phase-stability condition marks a practical limit on the idealized expression.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"equation\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"5\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Eq. 2\\\", \\\"Fig. 3(a)\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-ensemble-concurrence-readout--08064195a003\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Stored Ensemble Entanglement Metrics\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"Conditional readout efficiencies of 74% in free space and 84% in a cavity were realized for transfer from a stored collective spin excitation to a single photon. Heralded entanglement was stored in ensembles in distinct apparatuses separated by 3 m, and later work inferred concurrence C = 0.9 ± 0.3 for entanglement stored between L and R ensembles.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"These measurements show that the DLCZ-style architecture was more than a paper protocol: the necessary pieces of retrieval, remote storage, and entanglement quantification had laboratory demonstrations. The values do not establish a full repeater, but they ground the claim that primitive scalable-node functions were emerging.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"measurement\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"6\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Fig. 3(b)\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-microtoroid-coupling-metrics--08064195a004\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Microtoroid Coupling Metrics\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"For silica microtoroidal resonators, projected critical photon and atom numbers were (n0, N0) ≃ (2×10−5, 10−6). Quality factors Q = 4×10\"'^8 at λ = 1550 nm and Q ≃ 10''^8 at λ = 850 nm had been realized, with prospects for Q ∼ 10''^10, and coupling efficiency into and out of the resonator could approach 0.99–0.999 while remaining in strong coupling.\",\n      \"overview\": \"The microtoroid data identify one candidate path beyond conventional Fabry-Perot cavities. The metrics matter because large-scale networks need many integrated nodes with high input-output efficiency; the numbers also remain partly projected, so they support feasibility rather than completed network operation.\",\n      \"evidence_type\": \"model_constraint\",\n      \"claim_refs\": [\"2\", \"9\"],\n      \"source_id\": \"source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\",\n      \"page_refs\": [\"Box 2\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"evidence-eit-memory-transfer--08064195a006\",\n      \"title\": \"EIT Memory Transfer Results\",\n      \"excerpt\": \"Deterministic mapping of quantum states of light to and from atomic ensembles is possible by way of electromagnetically induced transparency. The cited work demonstrated storage and retrieval of classical pulses, extended the process to single photons, achieved entanglement between two ensembles coupled to a cavity mode, and realized reversible mapping of photonic entanglement into and out of pairs of quantum memories.\",\n      \"overview\": \"This evidence establishes the source'\"'s second route to reversible interfaces: ensemble memories using EIT rather than single atoms in cavities. It gathers a progression from classical pulse storage to single-photon and entanglement transfer, while relying on cited experimental achievements rather than a single new measurement in this review-like source.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"observational_result\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"7\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Atomic ensembles section\\\", \\\"Fig. 1(d)\\\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-density-matrix-growth--08064195a005\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Density-Matrix Verification Barrier\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"As moderately complex quantum networks are realized, it will become difficult to assess whether entanglement extends across the whole network. Attempting to determine the density matrix ρ(t) for the network surely fails because of the exponential growth in ρ(t) with the size of the network.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"The verification argument clarifies a limit that is independent of any one hardware platform. Even if nodes and interfaces work locally, conventional full-state reconstruction does not scale, so practical quantum networks require more targeted diagnostics or functional tests of multipartite entanglement.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"formal_argument\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\\\"8\\\", \\\"9\\\"],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\\\"Extending entanglement section\\\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"relations\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"supports\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"2\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"0\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Strong cavity-QED coupling is one physical mechanism for the reversible light-matter interconnects required by networks.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"derived_from\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"3\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"0\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"The dark-state mapping protocol instantiates the general reversible-interconnect requirement.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"supports\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"5\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"0\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"DLCZ ensemble entanglement supplies a second grounded route for distributing coherence between network nodes.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"supports\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"6\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"5\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"The stored-entanglement experiments demonstrate key laboratory capabilities presupposed by the DLCZ protocol.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"similar\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"7\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"3\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Both claims concern reversible mapping between optical fields and matter memories, using different physical platforms.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"supports\\\",\n      \\\"src_claim_ref\\\": \\\"8\\\",\n      \\\"dst_claim_ref\\\": \\\"9\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"The scaling barrier for verification motivates stronger procedures for heterogeneous scalable networks.\\\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"summary\\\": \\\"H. J. Kimble's The Quantum Internet frames quantum networks as systems of nodes and channels that require reversible light-matter interfaces, strong single-photon nonlinearities, ensemble memories, repeaters, and scalable verification. The source surveys cavity-QED and atomic-ensemble routes, emphasizing both experimental progress and the difficulty of extending and certifying entanglement in larger heterogeneous networks.\\\",\n  \\\"warnings\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"code\\\": \\\"missing_publication_date\\\",\n      \\\"message\\\": \\\"Publication date could not be resolved from the source record; date remains null.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"code\\\": \\\"date_signal_conflict\\\",\n      \\\"message\\\": \\\"Markdown contains an arXiv stamp of 25 Jun 2008 and a separate Dated line reading November 26, 2024, so the output does not infer a normalized source date.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"code\\\": \\\"review_like_source\\\",\n      \\\"message\\\": \\\"Several evidence items summarize formal arguments or cited experimental results from a review-style source rather than new measurements introduced solely by this document.\\\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"source_dossier\\\": {\n    \\\"summary_short\\\": \\\"This source presents the quantum internet as a network architecture built from quantum nodes, quantum channels, and reversible light-matter interfaces. It argues that genuine quantum connectivity provides scaling advantages over classically linked quantum devices and reviews two main physical routes: cavity QED with single atoms and atomic ensembles using DLCZ/EIT protocols. The piece is also cautious: memories, repeaters, local processing, error-corrected teleportation, and scalable verification remain major unsolved requirements.\\\",\n    \\\"summary_long\\\": \\\"Kimble's source treats the quantum internet not as a near-term communication product but as a research program for connecting quantum systems without destroying their coherence. The central architectural idea is that quantum information must be generated, processed, and stored locally in nodes, then moved or entangled across channels by interfaces that reversibly translate states between matter and light. This distinguishes a fully quantum network from merely classically coordinated quantum processors: when channels preserve quantum coherence, the accessible state space scales across all nodes rather than remaining a sum of separate node spaces. The physical burden then falls on light-matter interfaces. In cavity QED, single atoms inside high-finesse resonators can reach strong coupling, allowing a single photon and a single atom to interact coherently; dark-state adiabatic protocols show how a photonic qubit can be absorbed into an atomic memory and later re-emitted. The source pairs that route with atomic-ensemble schemes, especially DLCZ, where weak write pulses, single-photon interference, and heralding create a shared collective spin excitation between distant ensembles. It cites experimental progress in retrieval efficiency, stored entanglement over meter-scale separation, Bell-inequality tests, entanglement swapping, and EIT-based reversible memory mapping. At the same time, the paper's practical message is restrained. The demonstrated pieces are primitive relative to full repeater-based, error-corrected networks, and complex networks create a verification problem because full density-matrix reconstruction scales exponentially. The mature quantum internet implied here is therefore heterogeneous: it will likely combine atom-cavity systems, ensemble memories, integrated resonators, quantum repeaters, local processing, and new entanglement diagnostics rather than rely on a single uniform device platform.\\\",\n    \\\"sections\\\": [\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Network Motivation\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The source's first move is architectural: quantum nodes linked only by classical channels do not realize the same state space as nodes joined by quantum channels. This matters because networked quantum processors cannot simply exchange measurement records and retain the same computational or metrological possibilities; they need channels that carry quantum states or distribute entanglement with high fidelity.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"0\\\", \\\"1\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Light-Matter Interface Requirements\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The proposed network depends on reversible conversion between stationary matter memories and flying photons. Kimble makes the requirement concrete through controllable coupling χ(t), useful bandwidth κ, parasitic loss γ, robustness to imperfections, and error handling. The interface is therefore both a physics problem and a systems problem: memory timing, transfer fidelity, and loss budgets all matter.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"0\\\", \\\"2\\\", \\\"3\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Cavity-QED Route\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"Cavity QED supplies one candidate hardware path by enhancing the electric field of a single photon in a small, high-Q resonator. In the strong-coupling regime, g dominates γ and κ, and the critical photon and atom numbers fall below unity. Dark-state protocols then use shaped control pulses to absorb and re-emit photonic states while avoiding lossy excited-state population in the ideal limit.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"2\\\", \\\"3\\\", \\\"4\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Atomic-Ensemble Route\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The ensemble approach replaces single trapped atoms with collective excitations shared over many atoms. DLCZ uses weak write pulses and indistinguishable single-photon detection to herald a remote entangled spin excitation, while EIT provides a deterministic memory mechanism for mapping optical states into and out of ensembles. These routes trade deterministic local control for collective enhancement and heralded operation.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"5\\\", \\\"7\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Experimental Evidence\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The source grounds its optimism in reported milestones: efficient readout from stored collective excitations, heralded entanglement between ensembles separated by 3 m, inferred concurrence of 0.9 ± 0.3, Bell-inequality violation after memory readout, and early entanglement swapping. These are not full quantum repeaters, but they demonstrate several primitives needed for scalable nodes.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"6\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Scaling Limits\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"Kimble emphasizes that making larger networks is not just a matter of adding more working links. Verification itself becomes hard because full density-matrix reconstruction grows exponentially with network size, while purely functional algorithm tests may lack diagnostic value when networks fail. This motivates physical, scalable, and task-relevant tests of multipartite entanglement.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"8\\\", \\\"9\\\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Practical Interpretation\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The paper's practical stance is ambitious but not triumphalist. It expects heterogeneous networks that combine cavity nodes, ensemble memories, integrated resonators, repeaters, and error-corrected teleportation. The near-term value is in assembling and validating primitives; the long-term value is a platform for quantum communication, distributed processing, metrology, and controlled many-body quantum dynamics.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\\\"0\\\", \\\"2\\\", \\\"5\\\", \\\"9\\\"]\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\nprint(json.dumps(obj, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
